.TH SCS-CLIENT 1 "September 2026" "sslcrtsrv 0.8.4" "SSLCrtSrv Manual"
.SH NAME
scs-client \- register with an SSLCrtSrv server and request, list, and retrieve managed certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scs-client
.RI [ \-\-version ]
.I subcommand
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scs-client
is the client-side tool for the SSLCrtSrv certificate management system. It
registers a host with an SSLCrtSrv server, requests certificates for given
FQDNs, retrieves issued key/certificate/intermediate files, and can self-update
by downloading a newer client package from the server's catalog.
.PP
Every subcommand except
.B init
takes
.BR \-\-config " " \fIPATH\fR
(default
.IR /etc/sslcrtsrv/client_config.json ),
and automatically attempts an admin notification on completion, driven purely
by whatever
.I client_config.json's
own
.B notifiers
/
.B notification_routing
keys route for the outcome \(em there is no
.B \-\-notify
flag; with neither key configured, nothing is read or sent.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BI "init " "[options]"
Create
.I client_config.json
and the client's identity keypair. Options:
.BR \-\-client\-id ,
.BR \-\-server\-host " (default " localhost ),
.BR \-\-server\-port " (default " 5514 ),
.BR \-\-datadir ,
.BR \-\-registration\-secret " (prompted for interactively if omitted, to avoid shell-history exposure),"
.BR \-\-config .
Never wrapped in a notification, being a one-time interactive step.
.TP
.B register
Register this client with the configured server. Fires the
.B client_registered
event on success.
.TP
.BI "request " "domains..."
Register interest in a new FQDNs set (one comma-separated argument, or several
space-separated ones). Fires
.B client_certificate_requested
on success.
.TP
.B listcerts
List every FQDNs set this client is currently authorized for. Read-only;
notifies only on failure.
.TP
.BI "info " "fqdns..."
Read-only metadata for an already-authorized, already-issued certificate.
Notifies only on failure.
.TP
.BI "retrieve " "fqdns... [options]"
Fetch and save certificate data. Options:
.BR \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-cert ,
.BR \-\-intermediate ,
.BR \-\-combined ,
.BR \-\-all " (a path prefix producing " .key / .cer / .inter " files),"
.BR \-\-exec " (a shell command to run afterwards)."
Exits
.B 10
instead of writing anything when an existing, still-valid destination file is
already as new as the one just fetched \(em not treated as a failure, and
fires no success-event notification either. Otherwise fires
.B client_certificate_retrieved
when a file was actually written or replaced.
.TP
.B checkversion
Compare this client's version against the server's. Exits
.B 0
if they match or this client is newer,
.B 1
if the server is newer. Read-only; notifies only on failure (including the
"server is newer" case).
.TP
.B listpackages
Fetch and print the server's client-package catalog (name, version,
description) \(em no download. Read-only; notifies only on failure.
.TP
.BI "update " "[options]"
Check the server for a newer client package and, if one exists, download it
(and install it, if a command is configured). Options:
.BR \-\-update\-package " " \fINAME\fR " (falls back to " client_config.json 's
.B update_package
or
.B update_package_base
plus this install's detected package type),
.BR \-\-update\-command " " \fICMD\fR " (falls back to " client_config.json 's
.BR update_command ).
Fires
.B client_update_downloaded
(downloaded, not installed) or
.B client_update_installed
(downloaded and installed); fires nothing if already up to date.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A general failure (connection, authentication, or a subcommand-reported
error).
.TP
.B 10
.B retrieve
only: the existing destination file was already current, nothing was written.
Not an error.
.SH FILES
.I /etc/sslcrtsrv/client_config.json
\(em default configuration file, holding the client's identity keypair,
server address, registration secret, and optional notifier/update settings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scs-server (1)
.PP
The project's
.I Manual.MD
has the full narrative reference, including every configuration field and the
wire protocol this tool speaks.
.SH AUTHOR
Garry Glendown
